    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine which functions have inverses, we need to identify which of the given graphs are one-to-one (injective). A function is one-to-one if every horizontal line intersects its graph at most once. This means that for any input \\( x \\) in the range, the function value should be unique and no two different \\( x \\)-values should map to the same \\( y \\)-value.\n\nLet's analyze each function graphically:\n\n1. Function \\( A \\): The graph is a general cubic polynomial with local maximum and minimum values, and it intersects any horizontal line exactly twice. Therefore, \\( A \\) is not one-to-one because horizontal lines can intersect the graph at two points.\n\n2. Function \\( B \\): The graph is a cubic polynomial that passes through the origin and has a local minimum and a local maximum. This graph intersects any horizontal line exactly once. Therefore, \\( B \\) is one-to-one and hence has an inverse.\n\n3. Function \\( C \\): The graph consists of two straight lines that intersect at the origin and do not intersect each other anywhere else. Therefore, \\( C \\) is not one-to-one because horizontal lines can intersect the graph at more than one point.\n\n4. Function \\( D \\): The graph wraps around the \\( y \\)-axis infinitely and does not match any horizontal line. Therefore, \\( D \\) is not one-to-one because horizontal lines will intersect the graph at different points for different \\( x \\)-values.\n\n5. Function \\( E \\): The graph is a rational function that passes through the origin and has a vertical asymptote. The graph intersects any horizontal line exactly twice, so \\( E \\) is not one-to-one because horizontal lines will intersect the graph at different points for different \\( x \\)-values.\n\nThus, the only function that has an inverse is \\( B \\). Therefore, we need to enter the letter of the function with the inverse in the answer box.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{B}\\).",
