    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine which functions have inverses, we can use the definition of inverse functions: a function $f$ has an inverse if and only if it is one-to-one (its graph fails the horizontal line test) and onto (its codomain is the same set as its function's domain).\nFrom the graphs shown, we can see that functions B and A have the property that each element in their range corresponds to exactly one element in their domain. This means that each horizontal line intersects the graph at most once, so functions B and A are both one-to-one.\nFunction C fails the horizontal line test, so it does not have an inverse.\nFunction D is not onto, since there is no element in its range that corresponds to an element in its domain. Thus, D does not have an inverse.\nFunction E is the graph of a function that fails the vertical line test, so it does not have an inverse.\nTherefore, the functions that have inverses are $B$ and $A$.\n$\\boxed{B,A}$\nThe answer is: B,A",
