    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "When a function, $f$, is applied to any particular number $x$ it will return some new number, $f(x)$.  However, this new number must be unique.  Thus, $f(x_1) \\neq f(x_2) \\text{ for } x_1 \\neq x_2.$  This means that a function that is always mapping different $x$-values to different $y$-values will always have an inverse.  The graphs shown in figure 1, 2, and 4 all meet these criteria - no point on each graph have the same $x$- and $y$-coordinates.  However, the graph shown in figure 3 does not have this property - the point (0, -2) has been mapped to two very different $y$-values on the $y$-axis.\n\nThis leaves us with three figures that meet the criteria for having an inverse:\n\n\\boxed{B,C,D}",
