    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "When two graphs have inverses, one must be the reflection of the other across the line $y=x$.  Looking at (B), (D), and (E), none of them are the reflection of the others across the line $y=x$.  (C) is the reflection of it=self across the line $y=x$, so it is an isometry.  (A) is the reflection of it=self across $y=x$ and is thus an isometry.  Therefore, the answer is $\\boxed{\\text{A,C}}$.\n\nNote:  Answer mistakes result in a $\\boxed{27}$ point deduction from your test score.\nAs well as a warning-- there is significant potential for them to use trig to solve this problem with a proper solution.  I think it would be helpful to pass out TI-nspire calculators for some 3D math.  Otherwise, solutions from scratch and then with trig will give an overwhelming sigh of relief to all of the students.  Create a favorable environment for non-trig solutions!",
